<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
Cleartext policy for OcuBea.

The app is a camera server, and a camera server speaks HTTP on the local
network. That is the product. Turning cleartext off would not make it safer, it
would make it useless. So cleartext is allowed deliberately, in the smallest
possible shape: the loopback interface only.

Why loopback is the whole story here. Nothing in the app talks to a remote host.
Verified by reading every URL in the source, not by assuming:

  - ClipAdapter, ClipThumbs, MainActivity, SettingsActivity all use
    http://127.0.0.1:port for the app's own server.
  - OnvifSoap and OnvifDiscovery carry XML namespace URIs (schemas.xmlsoap.org,
    www.onvif.org, www.w3.org). Those are identifiers inside the SOAP envelope
    and WSDL, not fetches. Nothing resolves or dials them.
  - ONVIF discovery probes multicast on the LAN, which network security config
    does not govern at all.

So the only socket this app opens is its own server on 127.0.0.1. The remote
peers are the LAN clients the user points a browser or a VMS at, and those are
outside this app's control: the server binds a socket and serves them, which no
manifest attribute can restrict. Scoping cleartext to loopback therefore costs
nothing, and it stops the app itself ever sending anything in clear.

Deliberately not set:

  - A base config with cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" would be the lazy fix.
    The base here is "false" and only loopback is carved out.
  - No debug overrides block permitting cleartext "just for testing". That is the
    most common way a release build ships permissive cleartext, and this is
    exactly the kind of app where that matters.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <!--
    Cleartext is denied by default and permitted only for the loopback interface.

    The base stays false on purpose: this app is a camera server, it serves the
    LAN, and it never sends a request to a remote host. Verified by reading
    every URL in the source, not by assuming:

  - ClipAdapter, ClipThumbs, MainActivity, SettingsActivity all reach the app's
    own server on 127.0.0.1.
  - OnvifSoap and OnvifDiscovery carry XML namespace URIs (schemas.xmlsoap.org,
    www.onvif.org, www.w3.org). Those are identifiers inside the SOAP envelope
    and WSDL, not fetches. Nothing resolves or dials them.
  - ONVIF discovery probes multicast on the LAN, which network security config
    does not govern at all.

The LAN clients the user points a browser or a VMS at are outside this app's
control: the server binds a socket and serves them, which no manifest attribute
can restrict. So scoping cleartext to loopback costs nothing.

The <domain> entries below are hostnames, which is what a domain-config matches.
An IP literal is not a reliable way to exempt an address: measured on the
Android 6 phone (API 23), a request to the app's own server at
http://127.0.0.1:8080/status.json failed with

    UnknownServiceException: CLEARTEXT communication not supported: []

with these entries present and the base set to false. MainActivity therefore
reads its own status from StreamService instead of over HTTP; see
loopbackUrl() for why the self-request is not the fix.

Deliberately not set:

  - A base config with cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" would be the lazy fix
    and is the one change that would let this app send anything in cleartext to
    any host, to work around a self-request that has no reason to leave the
    device. It was tried, and rejected for that reason.
  - No debug overrides block permitting cleartext "just for testing". That is
    the most common way a release build ships permissive cleartext, and this is
    exactly the kind of app where that matters.
-->
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false" />
    <domain-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <!-- The app's own HTTP server, reached from inside the app. -->
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">127.0.0.1</domain>
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">localhost</domain>
        <!-- Android emulators reach the host's loopback through this alias. -->
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">10.0.2.2</domain>
    </domain-config>
</network-security-config>
